Q: Catch a thread&#39;s exception in the caller thread? A: <p>I love the <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/a/31614591/14900935">art_of_warfare</a> answer but I wanted to expand it to allow inheriting from the new class so I override the <code>start()</code> method to wrap either the target or the user supplied <code>run()</code> with the exception code. I appreciate that overriding the <code>start()</code> method is not approved by the documentation!</p>
<p>The Python 3 version:</p>
<pre><code>from threading import Thread

class ExceptionPropagatingThread(Thread):

    def start(self):
        if self._target:
            self._target = self.__wrap_run(self._target)
        else:
            self.run = self.__wrap_run(self.run)
        super().start()

    def __wrap_run(self, func):
        def wrapped(*args, **kwargs):
            self.exc = None
            try:
                self.ret = func(*args, **kwargs)
            except BaseException as e:
                self.exc = e
        return wrapped

    def join(self, timeout=None):
        super().join(timeout)
        if self.exc:
            raise self.exc
        return self.ret
</code></pre>
<p>Then it works in both cases:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>def foo():
    print(&quot;hello&quot;)
    raise Exception(&quot;D'oh&quot;)


t = ExceptionPropagatingThread(target=f)
t.start()
try:
    t.join()
except Exception as e:
    print(str(e))
</code></pre>
<pre><code>class Bar(ExceptionPropagatingThread):

    def run(self):
        print(&quot;goodbye&quot;)
        raise Exception(&quot;D'oh again&quot;)


c = Bar()
c.start()
try:
    c.join()
except Exception as e:
    print(str(e))
</code></pre>

Q: Why does cppcheck&#39;s MISRA scan complain about incrementing a structure member of a volatile pointer? A: <p>It is a MISRA violation and the reason is explained by the quoted text you posted - you can't have 2 side effects in the same expression. Accessing a volatile-qualified variable is a side effect and writing a value to a variable is also a side effect. The rationale for the rule is that multiple unsequenced side effects in the same expression leads to undefined behavior in C. Not an issue in this case but maybe in other cases.</p>
<p>Most likely the <code>pb-&gt;bar++;</code> won't be a problem in your application, but consider this scenario:  In case of <code>x++</code>, <code>x</code> is first read, then increased by 1, then written to. Now if <code>x</code> is a hardware register and a change to the contents happens between the read and the write, that change is lost. For an ordinary variable such a thing can't happen (unless multi-threaded &amp; there are race condition bugs), but in case of a hardware register that can change at any time outside the programs control, it is possible. The write will then overwrite whatever change that happened, which may or may not be an issue. Consider for example a 32 bit register with flags in the upper 16 bits and some counter in the lower 16 bits. A flag change by hardware would become lost.</p>
<p>The easiest fix is to drop the <code>volatile</code> qualifier from the function parameter. If that's not possible, then another easy fix is just:</p>
<pre><code>uint16_t tmp = pb-&gt;bar;
tmp++;
bp-&gt;bar = tmp;
</code></pre>
<p>It's kind of ugly but won't notably affect performance.</p>
<p>Another option is to deviate from the rule. In embedded systems there are plenty of situations where we might want to read a <code>volatile</code> qualified variable and write it to another one in the same expression. But since MISRA can't guarantee that doing so is always safe, they have a rule against it.</p>

